  "output": "I now have a complete picture of the codebase state. Here's the honest assessment, followed by concrete, actionable findings.\n\n## Context that shapes this report\n\nThis is a very early-stage scaffold (Phase 1 of the AGENTS.md build order). I read every implemented file in `web/` and `engine/`. The result:\n\n- **`engine/`** — `pipeline/blocking.py`, `pipeline/detect.py`, `pipeline/normalize.py`, `eval/generate.py`, `connectors/synthetic.py`, and `main.py`'s `execute_run` are **all `raise NotImplementedError` stubs**. There are zero loops, zero DB calls, zero matching/scoring logic to analyze yet.\n- **`web/`** — no client-side data fetching exists at all. No `useState`, `useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`, `setInterval`, or `addEventListener` anywhere in the tree (TanStack Query, mentioned in AGENTS.md for run-status polling, isn't installed yet). The console currently renders one real data screen (`/sources`), as a Server Component.\n\nSo most of the categories you asked about (N+1 queries in the resolution pipeline, re-renders in a polling conflict queue, memory leaks in subscriptions) **have no code surface to exhibit them yet** — reporting hypothetical bugs in `NotImplementedError` stubs would be fabricating findings. What follows are the two things worth reporting: (1) real, present-day issues, and (2) specific, load-bearing guidance for the Phase 2/4 code that's about to be written, tied to lines in AGENTS.md so it's not generic advice.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Real findings today\n\n**Duplicate query: `countSourcesBySide()` re-runs `listSources()`**\n`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // full SELECT + Zod parse of every row\n  ...\n}\n```\nHarmless in isolation, but the moment a dashboard calls both `listSources()` and `countSourcesBySide()` in the same render (likely on a future `/sources` summary), that's two round trips to Postgres for the same rows. Fix now, while it's a one-line change:\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return tallyBySide(all);\n}\n\nexport function tallyBySide(all: Source[]) {\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => SOURCE_KIND_SIDE[s.kind] === \"crm\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => SOURCE_KIND_SIDE[s.kind] === \"warehouse\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\nCallers that already have the list pass it in; only a cold caller pays for `listSources()`.\n\n**Unnecessary `\"use client\"` on `Button`**\n`web/components/ui/button.tsx:1`\nThe component has no hooks, no event handlers of its own — it just forwards props through `cva` and `Slot`. Marking it client-only forces every Server Component that renders it (e.g. `sources/page.tsx:19`) to ship it (and its `class-variance-authority` resolution) to the browser instead of resolving purely at render time. Per AGENTS.md's own rule (\"mark it `use client` at the leaf... only when it needs state, an event handler, or polling\"), this should be a plain Server Component:\n```tsx\n// no \"use client\"\nimport * as React from \"react\";\nimport { Slot } from \"@radix-ui/react-slot\";\n...\nexport function Button({ ... }) { ... }\n```\nSmall today (one component), but it's the kind of default that compounds — every `Input`/`Label` copied from the same shadcn template should be checked the same way before the console grows.\n\n**Missing indexes on tables about to be queried by foreign key**\n`web/lib/db/schema.ts` — only `entities` has an index (`entities_run_source_external_idx`, line 115). `matches`, `conflicts`, `resolutions`, and `audit_log` have none. Phase 4's conflict queue (AGENTS.md: \"filterable by severity and status\") will do `WHERE runId = ? AND severity <= ?` against `conflicts`, and the audit trail view will do `WHERE runId = ?` against `audit_log` — both sequential scans without an index, at exactly the row counts (thousands per run) AGENTS.md itself calls out. Cheap to add now, before there's data to migrate around:\n```ts\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_severity_idx\").on(table.runId, table.severity),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"resolutions_conflict_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n  index(\"resolutions_status_idx\").on(table.status),\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { ... }, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n```\n\n**Postgres pool has no `max` set**\n`web/lib/db/index.ts:38` — `postgres(url, { prepare: false })` is correctly cached on `globalThis` for HMR (good pattern, no action needed there), but no `max` connection cap is set. Supabase's pooler has a hard connection ceiling; in a serverless deploy each cold start opens a fresh client. Worth setting explicitly once you know the deployment target: `postgres(url, { prepare: false, max: 10 })`.\n\n## 2. Guidance for code about to be written (not bugs — design constraints to hold onto)\n\nThese aren't findings against existing code; they're the specific failure modes the stubbed functions are one careless implementation away from, called out because AGENTS.md already commits to the right answer and it's worth restating precisely:\n\n- **`pipeline/blocking.py::block`** — the docstring already says \"NEVER compare all pairs.\" The concrete trap when this gets implemented: embedding entities one-at-a-time inside a per-entity loop instead of batch-encoding both sides once (`model.encode(all_names, batch_size=...)`) before building the FAISS index. Same O(n·k) result, wildly different wall clock.\n- **Engine → Postgres writes (`db.py::write_entities/write_matches/write_conflicts`)** — these will be called once per run with thousands of rows. Use `psycopg`'s `executemany`/`copy` for bulk insert, not a Python loop issuing one `INSERT` per row — that's the N+1 pattern most likely to actually appear in this codebase, just on the engine side rather than in a React loop.\n- **`GET /api/runs/:runId/status` polling (TanStack Query, not yet added)** — when this lands, set `refetchInterval` to stop once `status` is terminal (`\"complete\" | \"failed\"`), or it polls forever on a finished run:\n  ```ts\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n    queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n    refetchInterval: (query) =>\n      query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" || query.state.data?.status === \"failed\"\n        ? false\n        : 2000,\n  });\n  ```\n  This is the one spot a real memory-leak-shaped bug (an interval that outlives the component's need for it) is likely to show up first.\n- **Ruleset evaluation (Phase 3, `agent/graph.py::classify`)** — AGENTS.md specifies rules are evaluated \"most specific first\" per conflict. If the ruleset (loaded once per run via `load_ruleset`) is re-sorted or re-filtered inside the per-conflict loop instead of once before the loop starts, that's a redundant computation repeated thousands of times per run for a ruleset that's typically under a hundred rules and doesn't change mid-run.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nNothing in the current tree needs urgent fixing — the two indexing/query items above are worth taking now since they're cheap, and the `Button` client-boundary fix is a 1-line change. The rest of your ask (re-renders, memory leaks, N+1 in the reconciliation pipeline) will become answerable once Phase 2 (pipeline) and Phase 4 (console polling/conflict queue) actually have code in them — happy to re-run this analysis at that point against real implementations rather than stubs.\n",
